A||N||46||FieroKyle||Spun out, almost wrecked my Fiero||||4||
Z||000000||FieroKyle||06-20-2001||12:29 AM||||I live in Michigan. I drive a 1985 Fiero GT. I was racing through the local park about 3 miles away from my house. It has like 5 connsective banked corners which i love to fly through at night because no one is ever. The reccomended speed limit of these corners is 20 to 30 mph. Of course i do things my way and go 70 to 90 mph. well I was at the final corner at 11:30 at night and I dived down into the corner (it was a left corner, my favorite), and the tires started to squeel like the do always (Goodyear Eagles, GT2s, 215/60/R14) well then the back end started to go on me and all of the sudden my car is sideways going 90, I turn right a little bit and got on my brakes little to may be get out of it and go. Well it didn't happen like that. I then tried and locked the brakes up. The backend just kept coming all of the sudden sending me into a viscious spin thinking the whole, I have really screwed my car up this time since there being guardrails and trees all over, anyplace would really damage the car if it went off. As I spun I could see I cam within a foot of hitting the inside guard rail. So now I am spun and the car starts to go backwards and slides up the banking, (sort of like when a NASCAR car spins out because they are too loose in the corner and back it into the wall). I come to a rest 1 foot from guardrail, but was too scared to get out and look at my car. I stopped so sudden and with like a banging crash noise, I thought I backed into the gaurd rail. The car was dead and took about 30 seconds to get it started and going again. At first it was so doggy driving, I thought I broke the engine. Well about a mile later the car started to be like its normal self. I went home, parked it, rush in for a flashlight and ran back out to the car. I inspected the car to find no damage or scrathes and my tries were not flat spotted. I so happy. This expierence has probably gave me one of my biggest rushes I have ever had after i found out the car was not broke and that spun out was awesome. These Fieros are one of the best cars I have ever drove as far as handeling is concerned. It is defintely a driver's car with the driver's actions controlling the car unlike todays new cars which are hard to have them break loose because they are setup to run really tight through corners.    ||||reg||4
Z||000001||Galen||06-20-2001||12:37 AM||||oh boy, I can see the 5-6 pages coming..<P>did you have your seatbelt on? were there any lil kitties in a box? Did you hit a lil ole lady and send her flying? Did you remember not to jump off a overpass? Remember, racing is deadly.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/angryboy.gif">      <BR>  <A HREF="mailto:galen@spinrpg.com">galen@spinrpg.com</A>   -<A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>1988 Hawaiin Orchid Coupe w/ sunroof</A><BR>AIM: GalenRPG - <A HREF="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/" TARGET=_blank>WebJolt Pictures</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Slammed Fiero||06-20-2001||12:40 AM||||welcome to the wonderful world of trailing throttle eoversteer.<P>you dove into the corner and probably , perhaps unknowingly lifted off the throttle.<P>I have done this 3 times at high speeds. all three on a track. Get a rear bar , it will make the transition from under to oversteer much slower... But still evident. To totally  get rid of this trait ..get out yer wallet.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">      <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals. &lt;b&gt; New White Face Gauges , 75Hp Shot of NOS&lt;/b&gt;Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000003||AkursedX||06-20-2001||12:40 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Galen:<BR><B>oh boy, I can see the 5-6 pages coming..<P>did you have your seatbelt on? were there any lil kitties in a box? Did you hit a lil ole lady and send her flying? Did you remember not to jump off a overpass? Remember, racing is deadly.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I was thinkink the same thing myself.  But racing is dangerous, but so are a million other things.  But if you can, at least try to keep it safe<P>BTW, welcome to the forum<BR><p>[This message has been edited by AkursedX (edited 06-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroKyle||06-20-2001||12:41 AM||||Yeah, I was wearing my seatbelt, but it don't help much in a fiero anyway, the gas tank is right under the seat and the car is made of plastic with no front engine to protect you. Sort of a Safety Nazi's worst enemy.<P>Kyle - 85 GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||Galen||06-20-2001||12:45 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>Yeah, I was wearing my seatbelt, but it don't help much in a fiero anyway, the gas tank is right under the seat and the car is made of plastic with no front engine to protect you. Sort of a Safety Nazi's worst enemy.<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>They are? I always thought they were between the seats and made of metal? maybe I got the special safe version of the fiero.. who knows..<P>btw, fiero's were rated 5 star side & frontal crash tests...||||reg||1
Z||000006||Shiner||06-20-2001||01:14 AM||||Uh, you probably shouldn't have gotten on those brakes buddy.  It makes a bad situation worse.||||reg||1
Z||000007||GarethWright||06-20-2001||01:39 AM||||Shure Nuff Galen, When they were made the only car safer (in this test anyway) than the Fiero was some Volvo, and only cause IT didnt burst into flames when... oops wrong article, here we go, only cause it got 5/5 stars in one category where the Fiero got 4/5, they tied 5/5 on all other categories.<BR>Welcome to the Forum<BR>Gareth||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierospeeder||06-20-2001||01:48 AM||||next time, get a 10 inch tall spoiler from a honda and you wont spin out.<P>Your car probably had fuel starvation preventing it from starting up.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ken Wittlief||06-21-2001||07:16 AM||||the Fiero would of gotten a higher rating than the Volvo, but the protection of the passengers in the rear seat was questionable, so the volvo got the 5/5.<P> Im interested in seeing feedback on what could of been done to get out of the situation. my instinct would be to steer into the skid (keeping your front wheels pointed where you want the car to go), and floor it, powerslide the car around the curve<P> but so far I have only started to spin my car once, and the pavement was wet from previous rain, and it was totally unexpected.<P>  I steered into it, and the car started to fishtail the other way, and i let off the gas and recovered - pretty much the same thing I would of done with a rear wheel drive car that was starting to loose it in the snow. But since it was unexpected I really cant say if I did the best thing to recover. (I wasnt anticipating it and responded more out of SnowBird Country reflexes)<P>   Its time for you to get your car on a dirt track, or a wet parking lot, and start figuring out how to control your car in a skid on a curve. The other response was correct - hitting the brakes was the worse thing to do (at least while you were still sliding sideways).||||reg||1
Z||000010||84Bill||06-21-2001||07:30 AM||||Just be glad your car did not end up like this. The previous owner of my Indy experienced the same thing but he hit a concrete divider instead of a metal rail.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>  <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/fiero/Indy/image0018.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-21-2001).]||||reg||11
Z||000011||Formula88||06-21-2001||10:35 AM||||Yeah, sounds like Drop Throttle Oversteer, but the brakes definately made it worse.  But, excuse me if I'm wrong, you sound like you enjoyed this - after you realized there was no damage?  Um, that's just stupid, IMO.  So you almost bought the farm, and made it.  That's pretty awesome!  I've had some similar close calls.  But you almost sound like you wanna go do it again.  Please tell me I'm wrong, here!<P>Either way, glad to hear you and your car made it through with no damage.||||reg||6
Z||000012||Mach10||06-21-2001||02:58 PM||||Fieros unsafe? Compared to say, a Mustang? <BR>Having the gas tank in the middle is, in my opinion, much safer than having it stuck under the rear bumper. If you get hit hard enough to rupture that middle-mounted tank, there wouldn't be much left of you anyway. Also, contrary to popular belief, most gas tanks will NOT explode if they catch fire. A tank will only explode if it's mostly empty. Gasoline doesn't burn, the vapors do. I remember my Mechanics teacher demonstrating this by donning gloves, lighting a match, and dunking it in a coffee can of gas. It put out the match. Then he did a similar thing with a long stick into a small container (small drink bottle) with a couple of drops of gas, capped, and warmed. Nice bang.<P>Having no engine up front means that the car has a larger crumple zone. This results in less injuries, to a point. No, the engine won't break off and hit you in the back of the head. Well, almost never, anyway  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000013||Galen||06-21-2001||03:55 PM||||Is that what hit me in the head? <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/devil.gif">        <BR>1988 Hawaiin Orchid Coupe  <BR>AIM: GalenRPG||||reg||1
Z||000014||84Bill||06-21-2001||09:08 PM||||No, that was me<P><BR>It does sound as if he enjoyed it but that can be just his way of dealing with it.<P><B>   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Gasoline doesn't burn, the vapors do. I remember my Mechanics teacher demonstrating this by donning gloves, lighting a match, and dunking it in a coffee can of gas. It put out the match. Then he did a similar thing with a long stick into a small container (small drink bottle) with a couple of drops of gas, capped, and warmed. Nice bang.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE></B><P>I dipped my finger in gas once and used it to light my smoke. Unfortunatly I did not know the tub water I was about to use to put my finger out with was gasoline. Fortunatly the fumes went before I got my hand in there. I would not recomend smoking it's really dumb.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <BR>Oh and it was funny to watch the garage going up while the song "house of the rising sun" was playing.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-21-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000015||p4n1c||06-21-2001||09:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 84Bill:<BR><B>It does sound as if he enjoyed it but that can be just his way of dealing with it.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Too true.  That was my way of dealing with a sideways slide down a gravel hill and almost hitting a rock cut.  I thought it was "awesome", it never hit me that I could've ripped the front end off the car.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||fyerstarter||06-21-2001||10:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroKyle:<BR><B>It is defintely a driver's car with the driver's actions controlling the car unlike todays new cars which are hard to have them break loose because they are setup to run really tight through corners.    </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>What are you an idiot!!! You spun out, LOST control of the car, and almost went into a gaurdrail!! Dumbass. The fact that your car is not wrecked is sheer luck, nothing to do with the car, and CERTAINLY nothing to do with your superior driving skills  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>The idea when cornering is <B>NOT</B> to break loose. When the tires break loose ****  like what you just went through happens, instead of completing the turn and being on your merry way.<P>Why do I get the feeling you just got your learners permit about a week ago?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000017||FieroKyle||06-22-2001||01:02 AM||||Further inspection of my car the following day before putting ball joints and a heater core in my car reveals that the left exhaust tip hit something when i spun out and lowered it 5 inches compared to the right which is snug like normal. I had to end putting a jack under the exhaust tip to bend it back up to where it is supposed to be. The exhaust tip nows like just like the right without anyway of telling something had hit it. <P>I was talking about the car, not about my driving skills, if I had better skills I wouldn't of almost put my fiero in the guardrail ******* . I am talking about like todays cars like have alot of roll to them when they corner and there are setup really tight, because the average guy doesn't know what the hell to do if the backend came around. The easy thing to do when your not gonna make the corner in a tight car is to slow down in most situatiobs. Unfortunatly I didn't make the right move and the back end went around on me in my fiero. But like when I drive my friend's new Monte SS, the car rolls more than it corners. Its like that with most new cars. Me and him tried cornering with it, not at high speeds, but good enough to get an assumption what it can do or not, and the stupid thing likes alomst rolls you outta the seat. But then again I like its power, but anyway to clear things up for you. Later<P>Kyle - 85' GT ||||reg||1
Z||000018||Galen||06-22-2001||01:25 AM||||Just dont melt that plastic car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> hehe<P>mr starter,  you really needa watch your language, you are constantly abusive to others, and 99.9% of the time have nothing constructive to say. You remind me too much of Cutter. Grow up<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/kid.gif">         <BR>1988 Hawaiin Orchid Coupe  <BR>AIM: GalenRPG||||reg||1
Z||000019||Kelvin Vivian||06-22-2001||01:32 AM||||About two weeks ago I brought my 1988 Fiero GT to the edge of adhesion and it was GREAT. So superb was it's handling.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I was about to enter a right-hand, looping 270 degree on-ramp when I noticed about 6 car lengths ahead of me there was a white 4-door Mazda Millenia looping the same on-ramp pretty quick... I thought, "I'll show him how fast "fast" is..." so I gunned my car around the loop in 3rd gear, started to catch him but he was pretty much through the loop already, as I got toward the end of the loop, I could hear the rear tires as they were just about to break, and just as they were breaking I counter-steered a tad and straightened her out... SMOOTH. So easy it was to bring her back under control. <P>I just got my 4-cyl SE out from the shop, had to replace the original clutch. I haven't sport driven it like the 88, but there is quite a noticeable difference between the two suspensions. The 88 just eats and soaks bumps up. But I do know that I need to replace the rear shocks in the 87, that'll help improve its bump handling abilities.<P>Be safe and watch out for boxes of kittens.<BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1601741&a=12208700&p=44402874&Sequence=1&res=high">  <BR>88 GT, 5-spd<BR>87 SE 4-cyl, 5-spd||||reg||1
